 ## 6. Drug Interactions
 
-Drug interactions with lithium orotate mirror those of other lithium formulations, primarily involving agents that alter renal clearance or enhance lithium retention [2][49][50][51].
+Most documented lithium drug interactions come from people taking high-dose prescription lithium, and it is not known whether they occur at supplement doses [1].
 
 ### Drugs That Increase Lithium Levels (Risk of Toxicity)
 
 | Drug Class | Examples | Mechanism | Risk Magnitude |
 |-----------|---------|------|:-:|
-| Thiazide diuretics | Hydrochlorothiazide | Enhanced proximal tubular reabsorption | ~25% increase in serum lithium [49] |
-| NSAIDs | Ibuprofen, naproxen, celecoxib | Reduced lithium clearance | 20–40% increase [50] |
-| ACE inhibitors | Lisinopril, enalapril | Decreased glomerular filtration rate | Variable; may require dose adjustment [51] |
-| Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) | Losartan, valsartan | Similar to ACE inhibitors | Variable [51] |
+| NSAIDs | Nabumetone (case report) | Reduced lithium clearance by the kidneys | Severe toxicity in one case on high-dose lithium [1] |
+| GLP-1 agonists (diabetes and weight loss) | Not specified | Not specified | Raised lithium levels, and some cases of toxicity, when started on stable high-dose lithium [1] |
 
 ### Other Notable Interactions
 
-- **SSRIs and serotonergic drugs:** Combining lithium with SSRIs may increase the risk of serotonin syndrome, though this risk is primarily documented at pharmacological lithium doses [2][29].
-- **Alcohol:** Not recommended with lithium orotate. Alcohol can potentiate central nervous system side effects (drowsiness, dizziness, impaired concentration) and, as a diuretic, promotes dehydration which impairs renal lithium excretion and may transiently elevate serum lithium concentrations [2].
-- **Dietary sodium:** Variability in sodium intake should be minimized. Sudden increases in sodium can lower lithium levels, while sodium restriction can raise them [52].
-- **Other lithium sources:** Combining prescription lithium with OTC lithium supplements creates additive risk of toxicity [2].
+- **Switching GLP-1 agonists:** Switching from one GLP-1 agonist to another while on a stable lithium dose may also raise the risk of lithium toxicity [1].
 
 ### Practical Guidance
 
-While these interactions are documented primarily at pharmacological lithium doses (169–338 mg elemental lithium per day), they are worth being aware of at lower supplement doses, especially in elderly individuals, those on multiple medications, or those with any degree of renal impairment. Anyone taking the drugs listed above should consult a healthcare provider before starting even low-dose lithium supplementation [1][2].
+People taking low-dose lithium should tell their doctor before starting or switching a GLP-1 agonist, and before taking an NSAID or raising its dose [1].
 
