 ### Regulatory Status
 
-In the United States, lithium orotate is classified as a dietary supplement under the Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act (DSHEA) of 1994 [2]. It does not require pre-market approval by the FDA for safety or efficacy. Labeling is governed by DSHEA regulations, prohibiting disease treatment claims, with permissible structure-function claims such as "supports mood balance" [2]. Manufacturing must adhere to current good manufacturing practices (cGMP).
-
-Regulatory status varies significantly internationally [2]:
-- **EU:** Novel food status under Regulation (EU) 2015/2283; not yet authorized as of late 2025, though a novel food application was submitted in May 2025
-- **Canada:** Classified as a prescription drug by Health Canada; not authorized as a natural health product or dietary supplement
-- **Australia:** Listed as a complementary medicine by the TGA, allowing low-dose formulations (typically under 5 mg elemental lithium)
-- **Japan:** Available as an over-the-counter supplement under general health food regulations
-
-The FDA has issued enforcement actions against companies marketing lithium orotate with unapproved disease-treatment claims [2].
